  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nAt 2 PM CDT, except for portion of the west river counties (edge of \ncloud shield, skies are cloudy due to earlier and ongoing \nthunderstorms over the region. Temperatures have struggled to warm \nup through the cloud-cover, with most of the forecast area \nregistering temperatures in the 60s and 70s. Those locations across \nthe far western CWA in sunshine have warmed into the 80s. Outside of \nthunderstorms, winds range from the southwest to the southeast \ngenerally in the 10 to 20 mph range with gusts to 30 mph.\n\nThe ensembles/deterministic GSM's all continue to point to the \nunfolding of this omega block pattern (a wave pattern change) by mid-\nweek as western CONUS energy/upper troffing works east across \nsouthern Canada/northern tier states and flattens the flow out to \nnearly zonal. This pattern change is forecast to hold into this next \nweekend.\n\nA more zonal flow pattern should knock the edge off all the \nwarmer/more humid than usual conditions of late. Guidance shows a \nsteady drop in temperatures from 80s/90s to 70s/80s once the first \ncold front pushes through the CWA Monday/Monday night.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are dissipating across north central South \nDakota (at the time of this writing). However, convective coverage \nshould increase across the far eastern CWA forecast zones from late \nthis afternoon through late this evening while the best mid/upper \nlevel forcing and lift overlaps the greatest instability/lapse \nrates. And the biggest concern/threat through this evening with \nconvection is heavy rain/flash-flooding potential. With very little \nin the way of deep layer shear, and sitting underneath the axis of \nthe upper level ridge, storm motion is only about 10 to 20 mph. So, \nslow-moving heavy rain-capable storms could pose a flooding threat.\n\nOtherwise, convective chances should take a break during the \novernight hours, returning on Monday (potentially as early as Monday \nmorning in the form of pre-cold-frontal showers/storms) when energy \nwithin the western CONUS upper trof lifts out onto the northern \nplains. Storms may be able to form in a pre-frontal surface trof \nover the far western Dakotas and race off to the east in the morning \nto early afternoon hours. A second round of storms may develop along \nthe actual cold front, itself, and move eastward into Minnesota \nduring the afternoon through late evening hours. SPC has the \nforecast area in a marginal risk for severe thunderstorm on \nMonday/Monday night.\n\nOtherwise, the forecast turns dry and cooler for much of the rest of \nthe week. PoPs return by the weekend when the next upper trof is set \nto work over the region. Still keeping a close eye on Tuesday for \nfire weather concerns (low humidity and 15-30 mph northwest winds \nwith higher gusts).",
